Based on the spec, any access to data0 (or any abstract‐command/data register) while an abstract command is already in flight must be caught in the DMI‐access path and raise cmderr.  That logic lives across:

1) The DMI interface (where reads/writes to DMI registers are decoded and dispatched)  
2) The abstract‐command FSM (which holds the “busy” state)  
3) The register bank (which exposes data0)  
4) The cmderr/status‐register file (which actually drives the cmderr bit)

All four of these will need to be coordinated: the DMI interface must check “busy” in the abstract‐cmd FSM before allowing a write into the register bank, and if busy, must set cmderr in the status‐register file.  

I’d expect the following RTL files to be touched (most important first):

```  
src/rtl/dmi_if.sv  
src/rtl/abstract_cmd.sv  
src/rtl/register_bank.sv  
src/rtl/status_regs.sv  
```
